{"name":"Into the Wild","hex":"#999885","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"into","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Preposition","definitions":["To or towards the inside of.","To or towards the region of."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/into"},{"term":"wild","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Untamed; not domesticated.","Especially, being of the wild type: being of an unbroken ancestral line of undomesticated animals, as opposed to being feral, being an undomesticated animal whose ancestors were domesticated."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Inaccurately; not on target.","Intended to be synchronized with film or video but recorded separately."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wild"}],"entries":[{"title":"Into the Wild (Krakauer book)","blocks":[{"text":"Into the Wild is a 1996 non-fiction book written by Jon Krakauer. It is an expansion of a 9,000-word article by Krakauer on Chris McCandless titled \"Death of an Innocent\", which appeared in the January 1993 issue of Outside. The book was adapted into a film of the same name in 2007, directed by Sean Penn with Emile Hirsch starring as McCandless. Into the Wild is an international bestseller that has been printed in 30 languages and 173 editions and formats. The book is widely used in high-school and college reading curricula. Into the Wild has been lauded by many reviewers, and in 2019 was listed by Slate as one of the 50 best nonfiction works of the past quarter-century."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/6/63/Into_the_Wild_%28book%29_cover.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Into_the_Wild_(Krakauer_book)","wikidata":"Q1235125"},{"title":"Into the Wild (novel)","blocks":[{"text":"Into the Wild is a fantasy novel about the lives of fictional cats, written by a team of authors using the pseudonym Erin Hunter. The novel was published by HarperCollins in Canada and the United States in January 2003, and in the United Kingdom in February 2003. It is the first novel in the Warriors series. The book has been published in paperback and e-book formats in twenty different languages. The story is about a young domestic cat named Rusty who leaves his human owners to join a group of forest-dwelling feral cats called ThunderClan, adopting a new name: Firepaw. He is trained to defend and hunt for the clan, becomes embroiled in a murder and betrayal within the clan, and, at the end of the book, receives his warrior name, Fireheart, after a battle with another clan. The novel is written from the perspective of Fireheart."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/7c/Warriorsbook1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Into_the_Wild_(novel)","wikidata":"Q2499667"},{"title":"Into the Wild (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Into the Wild is a 2007 American biographical adventure drama film written, co-produced, and directed by Sean Penn. It is an adaptation of the 1996 non-fiction book written by Jon Krakauer and tells the story of Christopher McCandless, a man who hiked across North America into the Alaskan wilderness in the early 1990s. The film stars Emile Hirsch as McCandless, Hal Holbrook, and Catherine Keener."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/d/dc/Into_the_Wild_%282007_film_poster%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Into_the_Wild_(film)","wikidata":"Q269912"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Alder_trees_beaulieu_river_fawley_ford.jpg/500px-Alder_trees_beaulieu_river_fawley_ford.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Alder_trees_beaulieu_river_fawley_ford.jpg/1280px-Alder_trees_beaulieu_river_fawley_ford.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Alder_trees_beaulieu_river_fawley_ford.jpg/500px-Alder_trees_beaulieu_river_fawley_ford.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The fictional forest of the novel was originally inspired by New Forest (above) and other locales in England and Scotland.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAlder_trees_beaulieu_river_fawley_ford.jpg"}],"sounds":{"into":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2b/En-us-into.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/2/2b/En-us-into.ogg/En-us-into.ogg.mp3"}],"wild":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4a/En-us-wild.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/4/4a/En-us-wild.ogg/En-us-wild.ogg.mp3"}]},"pictures":[{"title":"078 Wild Common kestrel in flight at Pfyn-Finges Photo by Giles Laurent","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/078_Wild_Common_kestrel_in_flight_at_Pfyn-Finges_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg/500px-078_Wild_Common_kestrel_in_flight_at_Pfyn-Finges_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg","ratio":1.5,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:078_Wild_Common_kestrel_in_flight_at_Pfyn-Finges_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg","share":0.469375},{"title":"Great Grey Shrike in Bhigwan August 2025 by Tisha Mukherjee 02","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Great_Grey_Shrike_in_Bhigwan_August_2025_by_Tisha_Mukherjee_02.jpg/500px-Great_Grey_Shrike_in_Bhigwan_August_2025_by_Tisha_Mukherjee_02.jpg","ratio":1.5,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:G